model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "The runtime adapter replaces the single pool controller instance every few hours whatever the load, and cuts every request in flight at that instant; a replacement must not fail an agent's turn. The model gateway entry treats the platform's reset text or its retryable flag, never an overloaded one, as a controller reset, and resolves the controller again for each send because a stub that threw this error fails again at once. Only an explicit allow-list of model routes is replayable, where a repeat costs at most the tokens of one response: messages, responses, chat and plain completions, embeddings, models and generate-content; a new route is not replayed until it is added. A route that starts a job or makes a paid artifact, such as images, video or speech, is never sent twice, because the provider may already have accepted it. Before the first response byte, a safe read (GET or HEAD without an upgrade, excluding video status polls) or an allow-listed model call is sent once more after one second. A model call's body is read from a tee beside the real send and held, up to 4 MiB, only until the controller answers; a larger body is sent once. A call whose first send ran past 20 seconds is not sent again, because a device call's signed assertion lives 25 seconds. The pool records each attempt on its own. A job-starting call cut before its answer gets the cautious unknown-outcome answer, so its client decides. A larger body, a late or second reset or a model socket gets the native retry advice: status 503 with an api error for messages and a server error for other model routes, with Retry-After of one second. It is never an overload answer, because the messages client gives up after three of those. A control write keeps its existing failure, because its callers may not assume it was not applied. A started stream is never replayed; a messages or responses event stream ends with the native retry terminal, an api error or a failed response, which the model clients retry by themselves, and a reader that leaves is not an interruption. Other started bodies and model sockets are still cut at this hop and are not recorded there. The shared answer for a model call whose outcome is unknown after dispatch is 502; on an allow-listed route it carries Retry-After of one second and no longer asks the client not to retry, and on every other route, or when the caller names no route, it keeps the cautious do-not-retry answer. Tool calls keep their not-replayed answer. Every failed controller call writes one POOL_CONTROLLER_CALL_FAILED line with the request identifier, endpoint template, attempt, elapsed time, closed message class and the platform's retryable, overloaded, remote and reset flags. Each event writes one POOL_CONTROLLER_INTERRUPTED line with count 1, outcome, saved (a replay the new instance answered successfully), reason, cause, method and endpoint template, and one pool_controller_interrupted observability adapter event joined by request identifier. Exception text, query strings and bodies never enter either."
      constraint "The real-workerd proof of the held copy sends any call whose answer leaves part of its upload unread last: workerd closes that connection, and a later call could start on the closing socket and fail as a client write error rather than as anything the pool did."
      constraint "That call is sent the way a full-duplex client sends it, reading the answer while its upload is still going: workerd answers before it reads the whole body, and a client that surfaces an answer only after its whole upload races the connection closing and fails with a write error the pool never caused."
    }
  }
}
